{
  "evidence": "TEXT_E: - Welfare-to-work: a single, payment-by-results Work Programme; reassess incapacity claims; sanctions for refusing work. - Lower and simplify business taxes; reduce regulation; make Britain open for business. - Transparency and payment by results in public services. - Protect real-terms NHS funding; patient choice; 24/7 urgent care; Cancer Drugs Fund. - Tougher knife sentences; elected police commissioners; Border Police Force. - National Security Council; renew Trident; restore the Military Covenant. TEXT_F: - Protect NHS budgets with legal guarantees on waiting times and cancer results; create a National Care Service with free home care for greatest needs. - More than halve the deficit via growth and fair taxes; 50p rate over £150k; bank bonus tax; NI rise while protecting the lowest paid. - Jobs or training guarantees; mandatory job offers after two years; extend tough-but-fair work capability tests. - Maintain police/PCSO numbers; use CCTV and DNA to tackle serious crime.",
  "reasoning": "The electorate’s highest priorities are proportionality, care, and authority; TEXT_E stresses proportionality through payment-by-results, sanctions for refusing work, tax/regulatory reform, and transparency, while still protecting NHS care and emphasizing law, security, and national duty. TEXT_F places stronger weight on expansive care and equality via protected spending, legal service guarantees, and progressive taxation, making it a somewhat weaker fit to the profile’s proportionality emphasis.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
